f the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "965cb62a-f834-5614-8e95-ef5ea2ae941c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069376 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069376 #edb/ref 1074 "The left Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is decreasing over $x\\in [-1,3]$ is the following. \n\n![](images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069582 #edb/ref 1061 652096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a126e5fd-355d-55ad-b4aa-e14b49d64d66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069583 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069583 #edb/ref 1061 652098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30e05a77-f052-5774-b6ca-d2d8db22d397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069584 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069584 #edb/ref 1061 652097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc16ac0f-77ae-504b-9997-be48eb6a1191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069584 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069585 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069585 #edb/ref 1061 652108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069585 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b3afc098-fc93-5ec7-9554-3989f6fd68d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069585 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069586 #edb/ref 1061 652109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069586 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd0626fb-e960-598c-8d94-fb531e7ccce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069586 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069720 #edb/ref 1061 652101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c489e48f-0e35-581c-85cc-0974d9100b36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069721 #edb/ref 1061 652105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4ba3e71-564e-5615-b646-afbf5d8127c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069722 #edb/ref 1061 652104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02dd1ff6-87f8-57cd-b528-ab8c67dc87b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069981 #edb/ref 1064 "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then  the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069981 #edb/ref 1065 4372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069981 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe559575-cc25-5a45-a1b2-fa3254bed0b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069981 #edb/ref 1067 "Overestimates and Underestimates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070005 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070005 #edb/ref 1065 727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070005 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "196ab182-655a-56ac-9e26-89a3d2835dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070005 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070006 #edb/ref 1064 "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg) on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) the area ![](images/6b/6bd69882ede929379fa9babdf49386dd09138f26cabc6a4d617bbac5e92d21a5.svg) under the curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) over the interval ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n![](images/c9/c92cf3627eeb4798f2f72577c3df4788c311b59b575ccbfa17caf14f39dacff1.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/4a/4a4a2bd99240362f13e0954449f58ab1c21ae7d29ffec2a1b2c8f1350f6fbec7.svg) and ![](images/09/0979b76b241c9ddb5f2e3d097811c00175e0f9242fa36cc4bff45e2963c3dd8f.svg) are the endpoints of the interval, ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the number of rectangles, and the step size ![](images/bd/bd77198ba2d349a9ae25a0866060aa94624d6e255cabe04e818dc06e755ae85c.svg) is given by\n![](images/c9/c9f5c5a2741bae825236da3287d754a2526954e894011ace34f5cde5e65a4b45.svg)\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the ![](images/a7/a7e281a725fb2bff766e9ddb72c85bb6f7a8e7d45de6212372795ba1764d5ddc.svg) which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/27/277114008d8847a523fb417695fe2f62a4f7ff08efc0b9b8ea58556029ea9c5e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070006 #edb/ref 1065 729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070006 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2ae388a-a2b0-584b-bf06-7b66f82ce253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070006 #edb/ref 1067 "A General Formula for the Left Riemann Sum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070007 #edb/ref 1064 "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070007 #edb/ref 1065 730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070007 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "86c2c7f1-e8cd-59f5-a5ed-1be84ec0a424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070007 #edb/ref 1067 "Left Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534269 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534269 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534269 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02a